NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"issue-1891-asynchronous-cleanup-oracle-review","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Issue #1891 asynchronous cleanup oracle review"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scope: the cleanup-settlement repair in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and the adjacent\nfocused refinements in\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-sqlite-persistence-core/tests/persisted.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":". This documentation\ncloseout does not change the reviewed semantic tree."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"claim-and-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Claim and limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Collection.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":" contract, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CleanupFn"},{"type":"text","value":" contract, and the\ncontributing glossary authorize the core law. Cleanup invalidates the old sync\nrun and starts local teardown synchronously. If that run owns adapter cleanup\nwork, the work settles before the Collection publishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":". The\nterminal status event admits a new sync run. A continuation attached to the\npublic cleanup Promise runs later."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concurrent callers receive one Promise. A rejected adapter cleanup still ends\nthe old sync run once, publishes "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":", and rejects each waiter with the\nadapter error as the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError"},{"type":"text","value":" cause. It does not retry the retired\ncallback. A later sync run remains admissible. Cleanup treats an incidental\nnon-Promise return from a contextual-void callback as synchronous. It awaits a\nruntime Promise-like return even when the callback is statically typed to\nreturn "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"void"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The core owner is a partial oracle. It does not establish full demand or replay\nhistories, provider transport shutdown, persistence-wrapper behavior, or\ngeneral row-publication laws. The adjacent persistence and PowerSync tests\nrefine the same settlement boundary for their wrappers. They do not expand the\ncore owner's row or transport claims."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The five oracle responsibilities are visible as follows:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the oracle opening and this record state the cleanup and restart\nlaw, authority, observation cuts, and limits."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" cleanup is a closed admission interval. Same-stack invalidation\nstarts it. Adapter settlement and terminal status publication end it. The\nterminal event admits restart before public Promise continuations run."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"History grammar:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the core matrix crosses abort versus release reentry,\nnested cleanup, and one versus two restart attempts. Focused cases add\nfulfillment, rejection, runtime return shape, concurrent waiters, and event\nversus awaited restart."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production driver:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the tests call the public Collection entry points. The\nadjacent refinements drive the real persisted wrapper and a real PowerSync\nNode SQLite database through public Collections and live queries."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refinement check:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the tests compare exact status, event and continuation\norder, Promise identity and outcome, error cause, callback counts, ownership,\nsubscriber counts, unhandled-error channels, and later restart behavior at\nnamed gates."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-011"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Collection.cleanup()"},{"type":"text","value":", the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CleanupFn"},{"type":"text","value":" documentation, and the glossary's cleanup and restart terms authorize the expected result. The oracle opening and this record exclude full demand/replay, provider transport, wrapper-wide persistence, and general publication claims."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The expected order comes from the public contract: adapter settlement, terminal status publication, then public Promise continuation. Held gates and sentinel errors supply the expected observations without importing lifecycle transitions, cleanup classifiers, or adapter settlement helpers from production."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps the contract and model in its opening, the bounded "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scenarios"},{"type":"text","value":" matrix beside the focused histories, public Collection drivers in each test, and exact refinement assertions at controlled checkpoints."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The repair makes no generated-history coverage claim. Its matrix is bounded enumeration, and the adapter refinements are fixed controlled histories."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Core tests call public cleanup, start, preload, and status subscription entry points. Persistence tests use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"persistedCollectionOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", same-resource replacement, and real coordinator ownership. PowerSync tests use a real Node SQLite database, source Collection, and live queries. Gates prove that public cleanup remains pending; exact status, error cause, call count, ownership, and global error-channel assertions preserve the promised violations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The R4 ordering mutant resolved the public cleanup Promise before scheduling terminal status. The gated core test reached the intended checkpoint and failed its exact order assertion with the public continuation before the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" event. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The mutant was fully restored. Pre-fix witnesses also produced early core restart, duplicate persistence ownership, skipped teardown, early PowerSync settlement, and unhandled adapter rejection at their intended checkpoints."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The repaired owner and adjacent refinements contain no important generated property."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable. The repair does not add, remove, combine, or split state in a stateful reference model. The bounded relation still distinguishes pending cleanup, terminal cleanup, and a later sync run because restart legality and public settlement distinguish those cuts."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The model-only phrase "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanup admission interval"},{"type":"text","value":" maps to the time from same-stack sync-run invalidation through terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" publication. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Sync run"},{"type":"text","value":", cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, acquisition lease, and Promise settlement retain their glossary meanings. Adapter cleanup settlement and public cleanup Promise continuation are separate observations."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize traces. Exact sentinel identity, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError.cause"},{"type":"text","value":", ordered observations, and unhandled-error arrays preserve the primary violation. Persistence uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleanupPersistedOracle"},{"type":"text","value":"; PowerSync uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withTestCleanup"},{"type":"text","value":". Both attempt every cleanup and retain secondary diagnostics. Core gates are released in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"finally"},{"type":"text","value":", and retired callbacks are not retried."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise. The core in-memory driver checks the public lifecycle relation. Persistence independently checks same-resource replacement, retired remote-owner fencing, and all-task settlement after a synchronous runtime failure. PowerSync independently checks eager provider disposal and already-acquired on-demand hooks through real SQLite. These paths share only the cleanup settlement and once-only release law; row ordering, projection, duplicates, and empty results are not part of this cleanup claim."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record. It identifies the exact reviewed\nsemantic head, records every ORC-001 through ORC-011 outcome, and is linked from\nthe coverage map."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"calibration-and-semantic-head-receipts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calibration and semantic-head receipts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The R4 mutant changed only terminal ordering. The driver had already attached a\ncontinuation and then released the adapter gate. Production reached adapter\nsettlement and both public observations. The exact comparison rejected the\nwrong sequence "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"adapter -> public continuation -> cleaned-up event"},{"type":"text","value":"; the\nrequired sequence is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"adapter -> cleaned-up event -> public continuation"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Additional RED/GREEN controls reached these missing laws:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An incidental numeric return from a contextual-void cleanup incorrectly\ndelayed core restart. Runtime Promise-like classification repaired it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A persisted source retired during synchronous entry later registered remote\nownership. A same-ID replacement exposed the duplicate owner."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A synchronous persisted runtime-teardown failure settled public cleanup\nbefore held source cleanup and skipped later release attempts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Eager PowerSync cleanup settled before held trigger disposal. The rejection\nlane escaped through the global error channel."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Active on-demand PowerSync cleanup settled before held hook cleanup, swallowed\na synchronous throw, and failed to await every reentrant release."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At the reviewed head, the core focused owner campaign passed 349 tests with no\ntype errors. Persistence passed 292 tests with one existing todo and passed its\ntype check. PowerSync passed 154 tests and its type, lint, format, and package\nbuild checks."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"known-limits-and-tracked-follow-up","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Known limits and tracked follow-up"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync cleanup now awaits eager trigger disposal and every already-acquired\non-demand hook. Two on-demand histories remain outside this repair:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onLoadSubset"},{"type":"text","value":" hook that is pending when cleanup starts and returns its\ncleanup callback only after retirement;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider trigger disposal created by that pending on-demand acquisition."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A held on-demand trigger disposer proved that public cleanup can settle before\ndisposal. A candidate that delayed terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" until that work settled\nmade the disposer checks pass, but violated the existing staged-subset baseline\nlaw: a held dependent preload could fulfill before source-cleanup publication.\nThe candidate was removed. This is a confirmed design gap, not green evidence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The durable destination is one core cleanup-start/intermediate signal distinct\nfrom terminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":". Adapters can use that signal to retire and await\npending or late hooks and on-demand trigger disposal without allowing a staged\ndependent preload to fulfill during cleanup. That destination must be designed\nwith the pending/late on-demand hook history; this review does not claim it is\nimplemented."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"dual-failure-reconciliation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Dual-failure reconciliation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"228b68f7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A later review found two sibling cleanup paths that awaited every teardown but\nreported only one rejection. The persisted wrapper preferred source cleanup and\ndropped runtime teardown. Eager PowerSync preferred the load-hook cleanup and\ndropped trigger disposal. The PowerSync loss was deterministic once both\nresources existed; callback completion order did not change the winner."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controlled pre-fix probes reached both production paths. The persisted probe\nobserved only the exact source error. The PowerSync probe observed only the\nexact load-hook error in both controlled completion orders. The repaired paths\nkeep source cleanup and trigger disposal primary, respectively. Each now uses\nan "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AggregateError"},{"type":"text","value":" to retain the secondary diagnostic. Lone failures keep their\nprior identity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The coverage gap was a missing dual-rejection history. Existing tests exercised\neach cleanup source separately. The persisted-history owner now crosses source\nand runtime rejection in one public cleanup. The PowerSync load-hook refinement\ncrosses hook and trigger rejection in both completion orders. Both tests assert\nthe public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SyncCleanupError"},{"type":"text","value":", aggregate cause, ordered error identities,\nonce-only callbacks, and terminal status where applicable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This reconciliation adds focused histories, not a generated-history or new\nstate-model claim. ORC-001, ORC-002, ORC-005, ORC-006, and ORC-010 are satisfied\nby the established cleanup contract, independent sentinel errors, public\nCollection drivers, recorded pre-fix assertion failures, exact error identity,\nand bounded resource cleanup. ORC-003, ORC-004, ORC-007, ORC-008, ORC-009, and\nORC-011 are not triggered by these focused refinements. This versioned section\nsatisfies ORC-012 for the reconciliation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"design-grammar-and-loss-audit-closeout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Design-grammar and loss-audit closeout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed semantic head: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This closeout adds the Query DB cleanup helper and public Query-backed\nCollection witness to the reviewed scope. It also re-audits the core cleanup\noracle, persisted wrapper, and PowerSync wrapper after the failure-retention\nrepairs. A later documentation-only receipt commit does not change this\nsemantic tree."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"intended-law-and-minimal-grammar","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Intended law and minimal grammar"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The retained model has five primitives:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An active sync run owns cleanup obligations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same-stack cleanup invalidation creates one shared retirement ticket."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An ordered obligation ledger waits for every acquired cleanup obligation."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A deterministic failure algebra preserves every failure, its role, and its\nownership order; a lone failure keeps its identity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The public observation trace is invalidation, obligation settlement,\nterminal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" publication, public retirement Promise continuation,\nand only then an ordinary replacement run."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The wrappers refine that grammar rather than adding unrelated Cartesian axes:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refinement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Primary role"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Later retained roles"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Core"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"adapter cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"local teardown"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"source cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"runtime releases in release order"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync eager"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"trigger disposal"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"load-hook cleanup"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"PowerSync on-demand"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"first acquired cleanup obligation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"later obligations in ledger order"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query DB"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"adapter cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryClient unmount"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A contextual-void callback may return an incidental non-Promise value; that is\nsynchronous completion. A native Promise or structural thenable is an\nobligation and must settle. Fulfillment and rejection both close the old run;\nrejection remains observable after terminal publication. Completion timing\nnever chooses the primary failure."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"loss-audit-retention-decisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Loss-audit retention decisions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The audit retained the distinctions that can change a promised observation:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"active run, retirement pending, terminal publication, public Promise\nsettlement, and replacement run remain separate cuts;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"zero, one, and multiple obligations remain distinguishable;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"incidental values, native Promises, and structural thenables remain separate\nruntime return classes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and multiple failures remain\nseparate settlement shapes;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"abort and release reentry, nested cleanup, and repeated restart attempts\nremain explicit grammar coordinates;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"one and multiple wrapper-owned resources remain separate because the latter\nexposes dropped diagnostics and order-dependent precedence;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"wrapper priority is a named refinement mapping, not an implicit scheduling\naccident."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The audit deliberately did not import demand replay, row publication, provider\ntransport, or adapter-specific startup into the cleanup grammar. A pending\nPowerSync hook discovered after cleanup starts belongs to the cleanup-start\nsignal design in PR #1897. Those behaviors may share code paths, but no cleanup\nobservation here can judge their full contracts."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"grammar-reconstruction-ablation-range-and-exclusion","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grammar reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconstruction."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Every known valid cleanup witness in scope maps to the five\nprimitives: synchronous and asynchronous cleanup, structural thenables,\nfulfillment and rejection, concurrent and nested cleanup callers, terminal\nevent and awaited restart, and each wrapper's resource roles. No known witness\nrequires a sixth lifecycle state or a wrapper-specific copy of the model."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ablation."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Each declared coordinate has a named contribution. Removing\nabort or release loses one reentry boundary. Removing nested cleanup loses the\nshared-ticket check. Removing the second attempt loses repeated early-admission\npressure. Collapsing return classes recreates the truthy-number or hidden-\nthenable bugs. Collapsing settlement shapes loses rejection ordering and lone-\nerror identity. Collapsing ownership count loses later failures. Removing a\nwrapper row loses its public resource mapping and precedence law. The\nexecutable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2 × 2 × 2"},{"type":"text","value":" admission calibration proves eight unique cells and one\nexecution coordinate per declared cell."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The bounded admission axes are abort or release, nested cleanup on\nor off, and one or two restart attempts. Focused margins cover zero/one/many\nobligations, incidental/native/structural returns, synchronous/asynchronous\nfailure, both completion orders, and one/two wrapper failures. No independently\nsupplied held-out marginal case was available after construction, so formal\nexternal range validation remains untested. A later range evaluation should\nsupply a behavior-preserving witness without changing the grammar first; the\nmodel succeeds only if that witness reconstructs from the existing primitives\nand the checker reaches its named public checkpoint."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exclusion and negative controls."}]},{"type":"text","value":" The grammar rejects restart before\nterminal publication, duplicated physical release, a dropped secondary\nfailure, treating a truthy non-Promise as a Promise, and ownership published by\na retired run. Pre-fix production reached and failed the intended checkpoints:\nQuery DB threw "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TypeError: result.then is not a function"},{"type":"text","value":"; persistence exposed\nonly the first runtime release error; PowerSync exposed only the first acquired\nhook rejection in both controlled completion orders. These were assertion\nfailures, not timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012-reconciliation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-012 reconciliation"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Outcome at the reviewed semantic head"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001: contract authority and limits"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The public cleanup contract and glossary authorize settlement, terminal publication, and restart. The oracle opening and this record exclude replay, publication, provider transport, and late-acquisition design."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002: independent judgment"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Held gates, structural thenables, sentinel identities, ordered public observations, and a role table derive expectations without importing production cleanup classifiers or failure selectors."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Contract and model are in the oracle opening; named axes form the grammar; public Collections and wrappers are the drivers; exact status, order, identity, count, and ownership assertions are the refinement checks."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered because this remains bounded enumeration, not a generated-property claim. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion are nevertheless recorded above, and unique-cell calibration is executable."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005: production path and observation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Core, persistence, and PowerSync drive public Collections. Query DB directly checks helper composition and separately proves the real public wrapper mounts and unmounts QueryClient exactly once. A supported public seam for injecting arbitrary async internal Query cleanup does not exist; no private seam was added."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006: checker calibration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. The prior R4 ordering mutant remains killed. New pre-fix controls separately killed truthy non-Promise classification and both same-role failure-dropping designs at their intended assertions."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007: fixed/random campaigns and replay"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered. No important randomized property was added or claimed."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008: stateful-model minimality"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not triggered by a state-model shape change. The audit still retained five observable cuts because restart legality and Promise ordering distinguish each adjacent pair."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009: vocabulary mapping"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Active sync run, cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, and Promise settlement retain glossary meanings. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Retirement ticket"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"obligation ledger"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"terminal publication"},{"type":"text","value":" are model descriptions mapped above to the public cleanup Promise, acquired cleanup work, and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"cleaned-up"},{"type":"text","value":" status event."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. Core and PowerSync hostile harness controls retain a primary mismatch plus every cleanup failure. Persistence now calibrates both modes: it aggregates cleanup failures when there is no primary and records all secondary diagnostics without replacing an existing primary. All actions are attempted."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011: independent second formulation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass within scope. The core public lifecycle uses a structural thenable; Query's direct helper isolates wrapper composition while the public QueryClient witness proves real mount/unmount wiring; persisted ownership replacement and real PowerSync SQLite hooks independently test wrapper refinement. Query's public witness does not claim arbitrary async adapter injection."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012: review evidence"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass. This exact-head record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-011, including non-triggered requirements and the unresolved held-out-range limitation."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"exact-head-receipts","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exact-head receipts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Environment: Node "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v24.19.0"},{"type":"text","value":", pnpm "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"11.1.0"},{"type":"text","value":", Vitest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3.2.4"},{"type":"text","value":", Darwin\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arm64"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At baseline head "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"efa90005248c7274795e489aa08e494914215483"},{"type":"text","value":", the new RED\ncontrols produced one Query failure, one persistence failure, and two\nPowerSync failures. At semantic head\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the core cleanup oracle passed 25 of 25 tests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query cleanup composition passed 8 of 8 tests, including the public wrapper\nwitness;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the selected persisted cleanup and harness histories passed 8 of 8 tests\nwith 240 unrelated tests skipped by the name filter;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"the PowerSync load-hook file passed 25 of 25 tests;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TypeScript checks passed for DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query DB's package build, including declaration generation, passed. Its broad\nTypeScript configuration also traverses DB collection E2E suites and failed\nonly because "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/electric-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" was not resolvable in this\ncheckout; no changed Query file produced a diagnostic;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync package builds also passed, and the\ndocumentation link check found no broken links;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"targeted ESLint reported zero errors. Its 22 warnings are pre-existing\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"require-await"},{"type":"text","value":" warnings in the persisted test file outside the changed\nlines;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prettier and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"git diff --check"},{"type":"text","value":" passed."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"issue-1891-asynchronous-cleanup-oracle-review","text":"Issue #1891 asynchronous cleanup oracle review","level":1},{"id":"claim-and-limits","text":"Claim and limits","level":2},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-011","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-011","level":2},{"id":"calibration-and-semantic-head-receipts","text":"Calibration and semantic-head receipts","level":2},{"id":"known-limits-and-tracked-follow-up","text":"Known limits and tracked follow-up","level":2},{"id":"dual-failure-reconciliation","text":"Dual-failure reconciliation","level":2},{"id":"design-grammar-and-loss-audit-closeout","text":"Design-grammar and loss-audit closeout","level":2},{"id":"intended-law-and-minimal-grammar","text":"Intended law and minimal grammar","level":3},{"id":"loss-audit-retention-decisions","text":"Loss-audit retention decisions","level":3},{"id":"grammar-reconstruction-ablation-range-and-exclusion","text":"Grammar reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion","level":3},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012-reconciliation","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-012 reconciliation","level":3},{"id":"exact-head-receipts","text":"Exact-head receipts","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Issue #1891 asynchronous cleanup oracle review\n\nReviewed semantic head: `21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20`\n\nScope: the cleanup-settlement repair in\n`packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts` and the adjacent\nfocused refinements in\n`packages/db-sqlite-persistence-core/tests/persisted.test.ts` and\n`packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts`. This documentation\ncloseout does not change the reviewed semantic tree.\n\n## Claim and limits\n\nThe public `Collection.cleanup()` contract, the `CleanupFn` contract, and the\ncontributing glossary authorize the core law. Cleanup invalidates the old sync\nrun and starts local teardown synchronously. If that run owns adapter cleanup\nwork, the work settles before the Collection publishes `cleaned-up`. The\nterminal status event admits a new sync run. A continuation attached to the\npublic cleanup Promise runs later.\n\nConcurrent callers receive one Promise. A rejected adapter cleanup still ends\nthe old sync run once, publishes `cleaned-up`, and rejects each waiter with the\nadapter error as the `SyncCleanupError` cause. It does not retry the retired\ncallback. A later sync run remains admissible. Cleanup treats an incidental\nnon-Promise return from a contextual-void callback as synchronous. It awaits a\nruntime Promise-like return even when the callback is statically typed to\nreturn `void`.\n\nThe core owner is a partial oracle. It does not establish full demand or replay\nhistories, provider transport shutdown, persistence-wrapper behavior, or\ngeneral row-publication laws. The adjacent persistence and PowerSync tests\nrefine the same settlement boundary for their wrappers. They do not expand the\ncore owner's row or transport claims.\n\nThe five oracle responsibilities are visible as follows:\n\n- **Contract:** the oracle opening and this record state the cleanup and restart\n  law, authority, observation cuts, and limits.\n- **Model:** cleanup is a closed admission interval. Same-stack invalidation\n  starts it. Adapter settlement and terminal status publication end it. The\n  terminal event admits restart before public Promise continuations run.\n- **History grammar:** the core matrix crosses abort versus release reentry,\n  nested cleanup, and one versus two restart attempts. Focused cases add\n  fulfillment, rejection, runtime return shape, concurrent waiters, and event\n  versus awaited restart.\n- **Production driver:** the tests call the public Collection entry points. The\n  adjacent refinements drive the real persisted wrapper and a real PowerSync\n  Node SQLite database through public Collections and live queries.\n- **Refinement check:** the tests compare exact status, event and continuation\n  order, Promise identity and outcome, error cause, callback counts, ownership,\n  subscriber counts, unhandled-error channels, and later restart behavior at\n  named gates.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. `Collection.cleanup()`, the `CleanupFn` documentation, and the glossary's cleanup and restart terms authorize the expected result. The oracle opening and this record exclude full demand/replay, provider transport, wrapper-wide persistence, and general publication claims. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. The expected order comes from the public contract: adapter settlement, terminal status publication, then public Promise continuation. Held gates and sentinel errors supply the expected observations without importing lifecycle transitions, cleanup classifiers, or adapter settlement helpers from production. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps the contract and model in its opening, the bounded `scenarios` matrix beside the focused histories, public Collection drivers in each test, and exact refinement assertions at controlled checkpoints. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. The repair makes no generated-history coverage claim. Its matrix is bounded enumeration, and the adapter refinements are fixed controlled histories. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core tests call public cleanup, start, preload, and status subscription entry points. Persistence tests use `persistedCollectionOptions`, same-resource replacement, and real coordinator ownership. PowerSync tests use a real Node SQLite database, source Collection, and live queries. Gates prove that public cleanup remains pending; exact status, error cause, call count, ownership, and global error-channel assertions preserve the promised violations. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The R4 ordering mutant resolved the public cleanup Promise before scheduling terminal status. The gated core test reached the intended checkpoint and failed its exact order assertion with the public continuation before the `cleaned-up` event. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The mutant was fully restored. Pre-fix witnesses also produced early core restart, duplicate persistence ownership, skipped teardown, early PowerSync settlement, and unhandled adapter rejection at their intended checkpoints. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with replay | Not applicable. The repaired owner and adjacent refinements contain no important generated property. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. The repair does not add, remove, combine, or split state in a stateful reference model. The bounded relation still distinguishes pending cleanup, terminal cleanup, and a later sync run because restart legality and public settlement distinguish those cuts. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. The model-only phrase `cleanup admission interval` maps to the time from same-stack sync-run invalidation through terminal `cleaned-up` publication. `Sync run`, cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, acquisition lease, and Promise settlement retain their glossary meanings. Adapter cleanup settlement and public cleanup Promise continuation are separate observations. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize traces. Exact sentinel identity, `SyncCleanupError.cause`, ordered observations, and unhandled-error arrays preserve the primary violation. Persistence uses `cleanupPersistedOracle`; PowerSync uses `withTestCleanup`. Both attempt every cleanup and retain secondary diagnostics. Core gates are released in `finally`, and retired callbacks are not retried. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise. The core in-memory driver checks the public lifecycle relation. Persistence independently checks same-resource replacement, retired remote-owner fencing, and all-task settlement after a synchronous runtime failure. PowerSync independently checks eager provider disposal and already-acquired on-demand hooks through real SQLite. These paths share only the cleanup settlement and once-only release law; row ordering, projection, duplicates, and empty results are not part of this cleanup claim. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record. It identifies the exact reviewed\nsemantic head, records every ORC-001 through ORC-011 outcome, and is linked from\nthe coverage map.\n\n## Calibration and semantic-head receipts\n\nThe R4 mutant changed only terminal ordering. The driver had already attached a\ncontinuation and then released the adapter gate. Production reached adapter\nsettlement and both public observations. The exact comparison rejected the\nwrong sequence `adapter -> public continuation -> cleaned-up event`; the\nrequired sequence is `adapter -> cleaned-up event -> public continuation`.\n\nAdditional RED/GREEN controls reached these missing laws:\n\n- An incidental numeric return from a contextual-void cleanup incorrectly\n  delayed core restart. Runtime Promise-like classification repaired it.\n- A persisted source retired during synchronous entry later registered remote\n  ownership. A same-ID replacement exposed the duplicate owner.\n- A synchronous persisted runtime-teardown failure settled public cleanup\n  before held source cleanup and skipped later release attempts.\n- Eager PowerSync cleanup settled before held trigger disposal. The rejection\n  lane escaped through the global error channel.\n- Active on-demand PowerSync cleanup settled before held hook cleanup, swallowed\n  a synchronous throw, and failed to await every reentrant release.\n\nAt the reviewed head, the core focused owner campaign passed 349 tests with no\ntype errors. Persistence passed 292 tests with one existing todo and passed its\ntype check. PowerSync passed 154 tests and its type, lint, format, and package\nbuild checks.\n\n## Known limits and tracked follow-up\n\nPowerSync cleanup now awaits eager trigger disposal and every already-acquired\non-demand hook. Two on-demand histories remain outside this repair:\n\n- an `onLoadSubset` hook that is pending when cleanup starts and returns its\n  cleanup callback only after retirement;\n- provider trigger disposal created by that pending on-demand acquisition.\n\nA held on-demand trigger disposer proved that public cleanup can settle before\ndisposal. A candidate that delayed terminal `cleaned-up` until that work settled\nmade the disposer checks pass, but violated the existing staged-subset baseline\nlaw: a held dependent preload could fulfill before source-cleanup publication.\nThe candidate was removed. This is a confirmed design gap, not green evidence.\n\nThe durable destination is one core cleanup-start/intermediate signal distinct\nfrom terminal `cleaned-up`. Adapters can use that signal to retire and await\npending or late hooks and on-demand trigger disposal without allowing a staged\ndependent preload to fulfill during cleanup. That destination must be designed\nwith the pending/late on-demand hook history; this review does not claim it is\nimplemented.\n\n## Dual-failure reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head: `228b68f7`\n\nA later review found two sibling cleanup paths that awaited every teardown but\nreported only one rejection. The persisted wrapper preferred source cleanup and\ndropped runtime teardown. Eager PowerSync preferred the load-hook cleanup and\ndropped trigger disposal. The PowerSync loss was deterministic once both\nresources existed; callback completion order did not change the winner.\n\nControlled pre-fix probes reached both production paths. The persisted probe\nobserved only the exact source error. The PowerSync probe observed only the\nexact load-hook error in both controlled completion orders. The repaired paths\nkeep source cleanup and trigger disposal primary, respectively. Each now uses\nan `AggregateError` to retain the secondary diagnostic. Lone failures keep their\nprior identity.\n\nThe coverage gap was a missing dual-rejection history. Existing tests exercised\neach cleanup source separately. The persisted-history owner now crosses source\nand runtime rejection in one public cleanup. The PowerSync load-hook refinement\ncrosses hook and trigger rejection in both completion orders. Both tests assert\nthe public `SyncCleanupError`, aggregate cause, ordered error identities,\nonce-only callbacks, and terminal status where applicable.\n\nThis reconciliation adds focused histories, not a generated-history or new\nstate-model claim. ORC-001, ORC-002, ORC-005, ORC-006, and ORC-010 are satisfied\nby the established cleanup contract, independent sentinel errors, public\nCollection drivers, recorded pre-fix assertion failures, exact error identity,\nand bounded resource cleanup. ORC-003, ORC-004, ORC-007, ORC-008, ORC-009, and\nORC-011 are not triggered by these focused refinements. This versioned section\nsatisfies ORC-012 for the reconciliation.\n\n## Design-grammar and loss-audit closeout\n\nReviewed semantic head: `ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6`\n\nThis closeout adds the Query DB cleanup helper and public Query-backed\nCollection witness to the reviewed scope. It also re-audits the core cleanup\noracle, persisted wrapper, and PowerSync wrapper after the failure-retention\nrepairs. A later documentation-only receipt commit does not change this\nsemantic tree.\n\n### Intended law and minimal grammar\n\nThe retained model has five primitives:\n\n1. An active sync run owns cleanup obligations.\n2. Same-stack cleanup invalidation creates one shared retirement ticket.\n3. An ordered obligation ledger waits for every acquired cleanup obligation.\n4. A deterministic failure algebra preserves every failure, its role, and its\n   ownership order; a lone failure keeps its identity.\n5. The public observation trace is invalidation, obligation settlement,\n   terminal `cleaned-up` publication, public retirement Promise continuation,\n   and only then an ordinary replacement run.\n\nThe wrappers refine that grammar rather than adding unrelated Cartesian axes:\n\n| Refinement | Primary role | Later retained roles |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Core | adapter cleanup | local teardown |\n| Persistence | source cleanup | runtime releases in release order |\n| PowerSync eager | trigger disposal | load-hook cleanup |\n| PowerSync on-demand | first acquired cleanup obligation | later obligations in ledger order |\n| Query DB | adapter cleanup | QueryClient unmount |\n\nA contextual-void callback may return an incidental non-Promise value; that is\nsynchronous completion. A native Promise or structural thenable is an\nobligation and must settle. Fulfillment and rejection both close the old run;\nrejection remains observable after terminal publication. Completion timing\nnever chooses the primary failure.\n\n### Loss-audit retention decisions\n\nThe audit retained the distinctions that can change a promised observation:\n\n- active run, retirement pending, terminal publication, public Promise\n  settlement, and replacement run remain separate cuts;\n- zero, one, and multiple obligations remain distinguishable;\n- incidental values, native Promises, and structural thenables remain separate\n  runtime return classes;\n- synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and multiple failures remain\n  separate settlement shapes;\n- abort and release reentry, nested cleanup, and repeated restart attempts\n  remain explicit grammar coordinates;\n- one and multiple wrapper-owned resources remain separate because the latter\n  exposes dropped diagnostics and order-dependent precedence;\n- wrapper priority is a named refinement mapping, not an implicit scheduling\n  accident.\n\nThe audit deliberately did not import demand replay, row publication, provider\ntransport, or adapter-specific startup into the cleanup grammar. A pending\nPowerSync hook discovered after cleanup starts belongs to the cleanup-start\nsignal design in PR #1897. Those behaviors may share code paths, but no cleanup\nobservation here can judge their full contracts.\n\n### Grammar reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion\n\n**Reconstruction.** Every known valid cleanup witness in scope maps to the five\nprimitives: synchronous and asynchronous cleanup, structural thenables,\nfulfillment and rejection, concurrent and nested cleanup callers, terminal\nevent and awaited restart, and each wrapper's resource roles. No known witness\nrequires a sixth lifecycle state or a wrapper-specific copy of the model.\n\n**Ablation.** Each declared coordinate has a named contribution. Removing\nabort or release loses one reentry boundary. Removing nested cleanup loses the\nshared-ticket check. Removing the second attempt loses repeated early-admission\npressure. Collapsing return classes recreates the truthy-number or hidden-\nthenable bugs. Collapsing settlement shapes loses rejection ordering and lone-\nerror identity. Collapsing ownership count loses later failures. Removing a\nwrapper row loses its public resource mapping and precedence law. The\nexecutable `2 × 2 × 2` admission calibration proves eight unique cells and one\nexecution coordinate per declared cell.\n\n**Range.** The bounded admission axes are abort or release, nested cleanup on\nor off, and one or two restart attempts. Focused margins cover zero/one/many\nobligations, incidental/native/structural returns, synchronous/asynchronous\nfailure, both completion orders, and one/two wrapper failures. No independently\nsupplied held-out marginal case was available after construction, so formal\nexternal range validation remains untested. A later range evaluation should\nsupply a behavior-preserving witness without changing the grammar first; the\nmodel succeeds only if that witness reconstructs from the existing primitives\nand the checker reaches its named public checkpoint.\n\n**Exclusion and negative controls.** The grammar rejects restart before\nterminal publication, duplicated physical release, a dropped secondary\nfailure, treating a truthy non-Promise as a Promise, and ownership published by\na retired run. Pre-fix production reached and failed the intended checkpoints:\nQuery DB threw `TypeError: result.then is not a function`; persistence exposed\nonly the first runtime release error; PowerSync exposed only the first acquired\nhook rejection in both controlled completion orders. These were assertion\nfailures, not timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths.\n\n### ORC-001 through ORC-012 reconciliation\n\n| Requirement | Outcome at the reviewed semantic head |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. The public cleanup contract and glossary authorize settlement, terminal publication, and restart. The oracle opening and this record exclude replay, publication, provider transport, and late-acquisition design. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. Held gates, structural thenables, sentinel identities, ordered public observations, and a role table derive expectations without importing production cleanup classifiers or failure selectors. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract and model are in the oracle opening; named axes form the grammar; public Collections and wrappers are the drivers; exact status, order, identity, count, and ownership assertions are the refinement checks. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not triggered because this remains bounded enumeration, not a generated-property claim. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion are nevertheless recorded above, and unique-cell calibration is executable. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core, persistence, and PowerSync drive public Collections. Query DB directly checks helper composition and separately proves the real public wrapper mounts and unmounts QueryClient exactly once. A supported public seam for injecting arbitrary async internal Query cleanup does not exist; no private seam was added. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The prior R4 ordering mutant remains killed. New pre-fix controls separately killed truthy non-Promise classification and both same-role failure-dropping designs at their intended assertions. |\n| ORC-007: fixed/random campaigns and replay | Not triggered. No important randomized property was added or claimed. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not triggered by a state-model shape change. The audit still retained five observable cuts because restart legality and Promise ordering distinguish each adjacent pair. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. Active sync run, cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, and Promise settlement retain glossary meanings. `Retirement ticket`, `obligation ledger`, and `terminal publication` are model descriptions mapped above to the public cleanup Promise, acquired cleanup work, and the `cleaned-up` status event. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. Core and PowerSync hostile harness controls retain a primary mismatch plus every cleanup failure. Persistence now calibrates both modes: it aggregates cleanup failures when there is no primary and records all secondary diagnostics without replacing an existing primary. All actions are attempted. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass within scope. The core public lifecycle uses a structural thenable; Query's direct helper isolates wrapper composition while the public QueryClient witness proves real mount/unmount wiring; persisted ownership replacement and real PowerSync SQLite hooks independently test wrapper refinement. Query's public witness does not claim arbitrary async adapter injection. |\n| ORC-012: review evidence | Pass. This exact-head record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-011, including non-triggered requirements and the unresolved held-out-range limitation. |\n\n### Exact-head receipts\n\nEnvironment: Node `v24.19.0`, pnpm `11.1.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`, Darwin\n`arm64`.\n\nAt baseline head `efa90005248c7274795e489aa08e494914215483`, the new RED\ncontrols produced one Query failure, one persistence failure, and two\nPowerSync failures. At semantic head\n`ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6`:\n\n- the core cleanup oracle passed 25 of 25 tests;\n- Query cleanup composition passed 8 of 8 tests, including the public wrapper\n  witness;\n- the selected persisted cleanup and harness histories passed 8 of 8 tests\n  with 240 unrelated tests skipped by the name filter;\n- the PowerSync load-hook file passed 25 of 25 tests;\n- TypeScript checks passed for DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync;\n- Query DB's package build, including declaration generation, passed. Its broad\n  TypeScript configuration also traverses DB collection E2E suites and failed\n  only because `@tanstack/electric-db-collection` was not resolvable in this\n  checkout; no changed Query file produced a diagnostic;\n- DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync package builds also passed, and the\n  documentation link check found no broken links;\n- targeted ESLint reported zero errors. Its 22 warnings are pre-existing\n  `require-await` warnings in the persisted test file outside the changed\n  lines;\n- Prettier and `git diff --check` passed.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Issue #1891 asynchronous cleanup oracle review\n\nReviewed semantic head: `21ed924352e91e47f0643de6d2542051ddd12d20`\n\nScope: the cleanup-settlement repair in\n`packages/db/tests/collection-cleanup-restart-oracle.test.ts` and the adjacent\nfocused refinements in\n`packages/db-sqlite-persistence-core/tests/persisted.test.ts` and\n`packages/powersync-db-collection/tests/load-hooks.test.ts`. This documentation\ncloseout does not change the reviewed semantic tree.\n\n## Claim and limits\n\nThe public `Collection.cleanup()` contract, the `CleanupFn` contract, and the\ncontributing glossary authorize the core law. Cleanup invalidates the old sync\nrun and starts local teardown synchronously. If that run owns adapter cleanup\nwork, the work settles before the Collection publishes `cleaned-up`. The\nterminal status event admits a new sync run. A continuation attached to the\npublic cleanup Promise runs later.\n\nConcurrent callers receive one Promise. A rejected adapter cleanup still ends\nthe old sync run once, publishes `cleaned-up`, and rejects each waiter with the\nadapter error as the `SyncCleanupError` cause. It does not retry the retired\ncallback. A later sync run remains admissible. Cleanup treats an incidental\nnon-Promise return from a contextual-void callback as synchronous. It awaits a\nruntime Promise-like return even when the callback is statically typed to\nreturn `void`.\n\nThe core owner is a partial oracle. It does not establish full demand or replay\nhistories, provider transport shutdown, persistence-wrapper behavior, or\ngeneral row-publication laws. The adjacent persistence and PowerSync tests\nrefine the same settlement boundary for their wrappers. They do not expand the\ncore owner's row or transport claims.\n\nThe five oracle responsibilities are visible as follows:\n\n- **Contract:** the oracle opening and this record state the cleanup and restart\n  law, authority, observation cuts, and limits.\n- **Model:** cleanup is a closed admission interval. Same-stack invalidation\n  starts it. Adapter settlement and terminal status publication end it. The\n  terminal event admits restart before public Promise continuations run.\n- **History grammar:** the core matrix crosses abort versus release reentry,\n  nested cleanup, and one versus two restart attempts. Focused cases add\n  fulfillment, rejection, runtime return shape, concurrent waiters, and event\n  versus awaited restart.\n- **Production driver:** the tests call the public Collection entry points. The\n  adjacent refinements drive the real persisted wrapper and a real PowerSync\n  Node SQLite database through public Collections and live queries.\n- **Refinement check:** the tests compare exact status, event and continuation\n  order, Promise identity and outcome, error cause, callback counts, ownership,\n  subscriber counts, unhandled-error channels, and later restart behavior at\n  named gates.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-011\n\n| Requirement | Outcome |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. `Collection.cleanup()`, the `CleanupFn` documentation, and the glossary's cleanup and restart terms authorize the expected result. The oracle opening and this record exclude full demand/replay, provider transport, wrapper-wide persistence, and general publication claims. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. The expected order comes from the public contract: adapter settlement, terminal status publication, then public Promise continuation. Held gates and sentinel errors supply the expected observations without importing lifecycle transitions, cleanup classifiers, or adapter settlement helpers from production. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. The responsibilities are listed above. The executable owner keeps the contract and model in its opening, the bounded `scenarios` matrix beside the focused histories, public Collection drivers in each test, and exact refinement assertions at controlled checkpoints. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not applicable. The repair makes no generated-history coverage claim. Its matrix is bounded enumeration, and the adapter refinements are fixed controlled histories. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core tests call public cleanup, start, preload, and status subscription entry points. Persistence tests use `persistedCollectionOptions`, same-resource replacement, and real coordinator ownership. PowerSync tests use a real Node SQLite database, source Collection, and live queries. Gates prove that public cleanup remains pending; exact status, error cause, call count, ownership, and global error-channel assertions preserve the promised violations. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The R4 ordering mutant resolved the public cleanup Promise before scheduling terminal status. The gated core test reached the intended checkpoint and failed its exact order assertion with the public continuation before the `cleaned-up` event. This was an assertion failure, not a timeout, setup failure, or unreached path. The mutant was fully restored. Pre-fix witnesses also produced early core restart, duplicate persistence ownership, skipped teardown, early PowerSync settlement, and unhandled adapter rejection at their intended checkpoints. |\n| ORC-007: fixed and random campaigns with replay | Not applicable. The repaired owner and adjacent refinements contain no important generated property. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not applicable. The repair does not add, remove, combine, or split state in a stateful reference model. The bounded relation still distinguishes pending cleanup, terminal cleanup, and a later sync run because restart legality and public settlement distinguish those cuts. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. The model-only phrase `cleanup admission interval` maps to the time from same-stack sync-run invalidation through terminal `cleaned-up` publication. `Sync run`, cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, acquisition lease, and Promise settlement retain their glossary meanings. Adapter cleanup settlement and public cleanup Promise continuation are separate observations. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. The tests do not shrink or normalize traces. Exact sentinel identity, `SyncCleanupError.cause`, ordered observations, and unhandled-error arrays preserve the primary violation. Persistence uses `cleanupPersistedOracle`; PowerSync uses `withTestCleanup`. Both attempt every cleanup and retain secondary diagnostics. Core gates are released in `finally`, and retired callbacks are not retried. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass. The named shared-fault hypothesis is that a wrapper starts cleanup but discards a returned Promise. The core in-memory driver checks the public lifecycle relation. Persistence independently checks same-resource replacement, retired remote-owner fencing, and all-task settlement after a synchronous runtime failure. PowerSync independently checks eager provider disposal and already-acquired on-demand hooks through real SQLite. These paths share only the cleanup settlement and once-only release law; row ordering, projection, duplicates, and empty results are not part of this cleanup claim. |\n\nORC-012 is satisfied by this versioned record. It identifies the exact reviewed\nsemantic head, records every ORC-001 through ORC-011 outcome, and is linked from\nthe coverage map.\n\n## Calibration and semantic-head receipts\n\nThe R4 mutant changed only terminal ordering. The driver had already attached a\ncontinuation and then released the adapter gate. Production reached adapter\nsettlement and both public observations. The exact comparison rejected the\nwrong sequence `adapter -> public continuation -> cleaned-up event`; the\nrequired sequence is `adapter -> cleaned-up event -> public continuation`.\n\nAdditional RED/GREEN controls reached these missing laws:\n\n- An incidental numeric return from a contextual-void cleanup incorrectly\n  delayed core restart. Runtime Promise-like classification repaired it.\n- A persisted source retired during synchronous entry later registered remote\n  ownership. A same-ID replacement exposed the duplicate owner.\n- A synchronous persisted runtime-teardown failure settled public cleanup\n  before held source cleanup and skipped later release attempts.\n- Eager PowerSync cleanup settled before held trigger disposal. The rejection\n  lane escaped through the global error channel.\n- Active on-demand PowerSync cleanup settled before held hook cleanup, swallowed\n  a synchronous throw, and failed to await every reentrant release.\n\nAt the reviewed head, the core focused owner campaign passed 349 tests with no\ntype errors. Persistence passed 292 tests with one existing todo and passed its\ntype check. PowerSync passed 154 tests and its type, lint, format, and package\nbuild checks.\n\n## Known limits and tracked follow-up\n\nPowerSync cleanup now awaits eager trigger disposal and every already-acquired\non-demand hook. Two on-demand histories remain outside this repair:\n\n- an `onLoadSubset` hook that is pending when cleanup starts and returns its\n  cleanup callback only after retirement;\n- provider trigger disposal created by that pending on-demand acquisition.\n\nA held on-demand trigger disposer proved that public cleanup can settle before\ndisposal. A candidate that delayed terminal `cleaned-up` until that work settled\nmade the disposer checks pass, but violated the existing staged-subset baseline\nlaw: a held dependent preload could fulfill before source-cleanup publication.\nThe candidate was removed. This is a confirmed design gap, not green evidence.\n\nThe durable destination is one core cleanup-start/intermediate signal distinct\nfrom terminal `cleaned-up`. Adapters can use that signal to retire and await\npending or late hooks and on-demand trigger disposal without allowing a staged\ndependent preload to fulfill during cleanup. That destination must be designed\nwith the pending/late on-demand hook history; this review does not claim it is\nimplemented.\n\n## Dual-failure reconciliation\n\nReviewed semantic head: `228b68f7`\n\nA later review found two sibling cleanup paths that awaited every teardown but\nreported only one rejection. The persisted wrapper preferred source cleanup and\ndropped runtime teardown. Eager PowerSync preferred the load-hook cleanup and\ndropped trigger disposal. The PowerSync loss was deterministic once both\nresources existed; callback completion order did not change the winner.\n\nControlled pre-fix probes reached both production paths. The persisted probe\nobserved only the exact source error. The PowerSync probe observed only the\nexact load-hook error in both controlled completion orders. The repaired paths\nkeep source cleanup and trigger disposal primary, respectively. Each now uses\nan `AggregateError` to retain the secondary diagnostic. Lone failures keep their\nprior identity.\n\nThe coverage gap was a missing dual-rejection history. Existing tests exercised\neach cleanup source separately. The persisted-history owner now crosses source\nand runtime rejection in one public cleanup. The PowerSync load-hook refinement\ncrosses hook and trigger rejection in both completion orders. Both tests assert\nthe public `SyncCleanupError`, aggregate cause, ordered error identities,\nonce-only callbacks, and terminal status where applicable.\n\nThis reconciliation adds focused histories, not a generated-history or new\nstate-model claim. ORC-001, ORC-002, ORC-005, ORC-006, and ORC-010 are satisfied\nby the established cleanup contract, independent sentinel errors, public\nCollection drivers, recorded pre-fix assertion failures, exact error identity,\nand bounded resource cleanup. ORC-003, ORC-004, ORC-007, ORC-008, ORC-009, and\nORC-011 are not triggered by these focused refinements. This versioned section\nsatisfies ORC-012 for the reconciliation.\n\n## Design-grammar and loss-audit closeout\n\nReviewed semantic head: `ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6`\n\nThis closeout adds the Query DB cleanup helper and public Query-backed\nCollection witness to the reviewed scope. It also re-audits the core cleanup\noracle, persisted wrapper, and PowerSync wrapper after the failure-retention\nrepairs. A later documentation-only receipt commit does not change this\nsemantic tree.\n\n### Intended law and minimal grammar\n\nThe retained model has five primitives:\n\n1. An active sync run owns cleanup obligations.\n2. Same-stack cleanup invalidation creates one shared retirement ticket.\n3. An ordered obligation ledger waits for every acquired cleanup obligation.\n4. A deterministic failure algebra preserves every failure, its role, and its\n   ownership order; a lone failure keeps its identity.\n5. The public observation trace is invalidation, obligation settlement,\n   terminal `cleaned-up` publication, public retirement Promise continuation,\n   and only then an ordinary replacement run.\n\nThe wrappers refine that grammar rather than adding unrelated Cartesian axes:\n\n| Refinement | Primary role | Later retained roles |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Core | adapter cleanup | local teardown |\n| Persistence | source cleanup | runtime releases in release order |\n| PowerSync eager | trigger disposal | load-hook cleanup |\n| PowerSync on-demand | first acquired cleanup obligation | later obligations in ledger order |\n| Query DB | adapter cleanup | QueryClient unmount |\n\nA contextual-void callback may return an incidental non-Promise value; that is\nsynchronous completion. A native Promise or structural thenable is an\nobligation and must settle. Fulfillment and rejection both close the old run;\nrejection remains observable after terminal publication. Completion timing\nnever chooses the primary failure.\n\n### Loss-audit retention decisions\n\nThe audit retained the distinctions that can change a promised observation:\n\n- active run, retirement pending, terminal publication, public Promise\n  settlement, and replacement run remain separate cuts;\n- zero, one, and multiple obligations remain distinguishable;\n- incidental values, native Promises, and structural thenables remain separate\n  runtime return classes;\n- synchronous throw, asynchronous rejection, and multiple failures remain\n  separate settlement shapes;\n- abort and release reentry, nested cleanup, and repeated restart attempts\n  remain explicit grammar coordinates;\n- one and multiple wrapper-owned resources remain separate because the latter\n  exposes dropped diagnostics and order-dependent precedence;\n- wrapper priority is a named refinement mapping, not an implicit scheduling\n  accident.\n\nThe audit deliberately did not import demand replay, row publication, provider\ntransport, or adapter-specific startup into the cleanup grammar. A pending\nPowerSync hook discovered after cleanup starts belongs to the cleanup-start\nsignal design in PR #1897. Those behaviors may share code paths, but no cleanup\nobservation here can judge their full contracts.\n\n### Grammar reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion\n\n**Reconstruction.** Every known valid cleanup witness in scope maps to the five\nprimitives: synchronous and asynchronous cleanup, structural thenables,\nfulfillment and rejection, concurrent and nested cleanup callers, terminal\nevent and awaited restart, and each wrapper's resource roles. No known witness\nrequires a sixth lifecycle state or a wrapper-specific copy of the model.\n\n**Ablation.** Each declared coordinate has a named contribution. Removing\nabort or release loses one reentry boundary. Removing nested cleanup loses the\nshared-ticket check. Removing the second attempt loses repeated early-admission\npressure. Collapsing return classes recreates the truthy-number or hidden-\nthenable bugs. Collapsing settlement shapes loses rejection ordering and lone-\nerror identity. Collapsing ownership count loses later failures. Removing a\nwrapper row loses its public resource mapping and precedence law. The\nexecutable `2 × 2 × 2` admission calibration proves eight unique cells and one\nexecution coordinate per declared cell.\n\n**Range.** The bounded admission axes are abort or release, nested cleanup on\nor off, and one or two restart attempts. Focused margins cover zero/one/many\nobligations, incidental/native/structural returns, synchronous/asynchronous\nfailure, both completion orders, and one/two wrapper failures. No independently\nsupplied held-out marginal case was available after construction, so formal\nexternal range validation remains untested. A later range evaluation should\nsupply a behavior-preserving witness without changing the grammar first; the\nmodel succeeds only if that witness reconstructs from the existing primitives\nand the checker reaches its named public checkpoint.\n\n**Exclusion and negative controls.** The grammar rejects restart before\nterminal publication, duplicated physical release, a dropped secondary\nfailure, treating a truthy non-Promise as a Promise, and ownership published by\na retired run. Pre-fix production reached and failed the intended checkpoints:\nQuery DB threw `TypeError: result.then is not a function`; persistence exposed\nonly the first runtime release error; PowerSync exposed only the first acquired\nhook rejection in both controlled completion orders. These were assertion\nfailures, not timeouts, setup failures, or unreached paths.\n\n### ORC-001 through ORC-012 reconciliation\n\n| Requirement | Outcome at the reviewed semantic head |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001: contract authority and limits | Pass. The public cleanup contract and glossary authorize settlement, terminal publication, and restart. The oracle opening and this record exclude replay, publication, provider transport, and late-acquisition design. |\n| ORC-002: independent judgment | Pass. Held gates, structural thenables, sentinel identities, ordered public observations, and a role table derive expectations without importing production cleanup classifiers or failure selectors. |\n| ORC-003: distinguishable responsibilities | Pass. Contract and model are in the oracle opening; named axes form the grammar; public Collections and wrappers are the drivers; exact status, order, identity, count, and ownership assertions are the refinement checks. |\n| ORC-004: generated-history grammar controls | Not triggered because this remains bounded enumeration, not a generated-property claim. Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion are nevertheless recorded above, and unique-cell calibration is executable. |\n| ORC-005: production path and observation | Pass. Core, persistence, and PowerSync drive public Collections. Query DB directly checks helper composition and separately proves the real public wrapper mounts and unmounts QueryClient exactly once. A supported public seam for injecting arbitrary async internal Query cleanup does not exist; no private seam was added. |\n| ORC-006: checker calibration | Pass. The prior R4 ordering mutant remains killed. New pre-fix controls separately killed truthy non-Promise classification and both same-role failure-dropping designs at their intended assertions. |\n| ORC-007: fixed/random campaigns and replay | Not triggered. No important randomized property was added or claimed. |\n| ORC-008: stateful-model minimality | Not triggered by a state-model shape change. The audit still retained five observable cuts because restart legality and Promise ordering distinguish each adjacent pair. |\n| ORC-009: vocabulary mapping | Pass. Active sync run, cleanup, restart, logical owner, physical acquisition, and Promise settlement retain glossary meanings. `Retirement ticket`, `obligation ledger`, and `terminal publication` are model descriptions mapped above to the public cleanup Promise, acquired cleanup work, and the `cleaned-up` status event. |\n| ORC-010: failure fidelity and cleanup | Pass. Core and PowerSync hostile harness controls retain a primary mismatch plus every cleanup failure. Persistence now calibrates both modes: it aggregates cleanup failures when there is no primary and records all secondary diagnostics without replacing an existing primary. All actions are attempted. |\n| ORC-011: independent second formulation | Pass within scope. The core public lifecycle uses a structural thenable; Query's direct helper isolates wrapper composition while the public QueryClient witness proves real mount/unmount wiring; persisted ownership replacement and real PowerSync SQLite hooks independently test wrapper refinement. Query's public witness does not claim arbitrary async adapter injection. |\n| ORC-012: review evidence | Pass. This exact-head record accounts for ORC-001 through ORC-011, including non-triggered requirements and the unresolved held-out-range limitation. |\n\n### Exact-head receipts\n\nEnvironment: Node `v24.19.0`, pnpm `11.1.0`, Vitest `3.2.4`, Darwin\n`arm64`.\n\nAt baseline head `efa90005248c7274795e489aa08e494914215483`, the new RED\ncontrols produced one Query failure, one persistence failure, and two\nPowerSync failures. At semantic head\n`ccef1e2ea6f6ea04740a27d7de6efde3424ecca6`:\n\n- the core cleanup oracle passed 25 of 25 tests;\n- Query cleanup composition passed 8 of 8 tests, including the public wrapper\n  witness;\n- the selected persisted cleanup and harness histories passed 8 of 8 tests\n  with 240 unrelated tests skipped by the name filter;\n- the PowerSync load-hook file passed 25 of 25 tests;\n- TypeScript checks passed for DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync;\n- Query DB's package build, including declaration generation, passed. Its broad\n  TypeScript configuration also traverses DB collection E2E suites and failed\n  only because `@tanstack/electric-db-collection` was not resolvable in this\n  checkout; no changed Query file produced a diagnostic;\n- DB, SQLite persistence, and PowerSync package builds also passed, and the\n  documentation link check found no broken links;\n- targeted ESLint reported zero errors. Its 22 warnings are pre-existing\n  `require-await` warnings in the persisted test file outside the changed\n  lines;\n- Prettier and `git diff --check` passed.\n"}
